#
# One fingerprint per line, never a path or directory rule. A directory
# exemption would silence every future finding under it as well, which is how a
# real credential gets committed into a folder someone once excused. Each entry
# below names an exact commit, file, rule and line, so it stops applying the
# moment that line changes.
#
# Every exemption here is a documentation or test placeholder verified by hand
# at the line cited. Adding one requires reading the line, not trusting this
# comment.

# scripts/privacy_guard.py builds its own negative fixtures as SPLIT string
# literals -- `"p" + 'assword": "secure_password"'` -- precisely so the guard's
# own patterns do not match its test data. gitleaks reassembles the
# concatenation and sees a credential assignment that exists in no single
# literal. The value is the word "secure_password".
#
# THIS ENTRY CANNOT BE REMOVED, and the reason is worth stating because the same
# finding was raised in review four times. `gitleaks git` scans DIFFS and
# attributes a secret to the commit that ADDED the line, so this fingerprint is
# pinned to `03cb443`, which is an immutable ancestor of every future commit on
# this branch. Nothing in the present tree can change it and no edit can make it
# stale -- which also means it can never DRIFT, unlike a working-tree entry.
#
# What was removable was the cause of new findings: the fixture that produced the
# working-tree entry has been re-split so gitleaks no longer matches it at all
# (see the note at that line). `scripts/privacy_guard.py` therefore has no
# working-tree fingerprint below, and no future edit to it can create one.
