# --- working-tree fingerprints ---
#
# The CI job runs BOTH `gitleaks git` (history) and `gitleaks dir` (the tree),
# and the two produce DIFFERENT fingerprint forms: history entries carry a
# commit prefix, working-tree entries do not. Listing only the history form
# left the `dir .` scan failing with the identical findings. The line
# numbers also differ between the two, because history pins the line as it was
# in that commit and the tree pins it as it is now.
#
# A working-tree fingerprint is pinned to a CURRENT line number, so it stops
# matching the moment anything above that line grows or shrinks -- even when the
# excused line itself is untouched. Editing `scripts/privacy_guard.py` shifted
# its fixture by 32 lines and turned the `dir` scan red while the history scan
# stayed green and the local pre-commit hook, which scans only the staged diff,
# passed. Local green does not predict CI green here.
#
# `tests/test_repo_hygiene.py::GitleaksSuppressionTests` now fails when an entry
# drifts onto a line that could not produce the rule it names, which is what
# turns this from a CI surprise into a local one. It cannot prove an entry is
# RIGHT -- only gitleaks can -- but drift is the failure that actually happens.
#
# The best fix for a drifting entry is not a better entry: it is not needing one.
# `scripts/privacy_guard.py` used to appear here and no longer does, because its
# fixture was re-split so gitleaks stops matching it while the runtime value stays
# byte-identical. The five below are absorbed vendor documents whose sample text
# must keep looking like the credential it warns about, so they cannot be
# rewritten the same way and are exempted per line instead.
#
# Five placeholders, in documents this repository absorbed rather than wrote,
# verified at the lines cited.
